<p>President Trump and Brazilian President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva met for about three hours at the White House on Thursday, with both leaders describing the talks as positive after months of disputes over tariffs, regional policy and the legal case involving Jair Bolsonaro. The meeting was a working session rather than a state visit, and a planned joint press appearance did not take place. Trump said on Truth Social that the two discussed “many topics, including Trade and, specifically, Tariffs,” and called Lula “the very dynamic President of Brazil”. Lula said afterward that the meeting was “a step forward” in consolidating Brazil-U.S. relations, and said Brazil would not give up its democracy or sovereignty. The agenda included tariffs, trade, security cooperation, organized crime, critical minerals and rare earths. Lula told reporters that Brazil’s rare earth reserves were open to investors from China, the United States or any other country willing to process minerals inside Brazil, and said Trump told him the United States was not planning to invade Cuba.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/utwqIGAt7MUVObUG1plW-RFQ0kGnbRcD9qImqyFP3Y1AnJQi25XAW5DeiFxY0mN_Xhilh2ocg_rw15_VvGT4ZaEyXFrfbcb2H2f8HjSmj52LgKNqLMahpgmCbpZe5Ov7C9gctJ1a0Sv8KbM5wODTs6XxGcAz1_c3pnFcPMJVnsrUOIgqcW7f1N7MCZsKpg7Bn1yXzz_hn-vS9u-EApTV6bkSMhh1LDmcpSRNsyMBYackg39_kzu-zhvhJGkeX1B9az8jLxfIusB2BDhvSw' alt='Brazilian President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva speaks during a press conference at the Brazilian Embassy after meeting President Trump at the White House.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Tariff talks: Lula said he proposed a Brazil-U.S. working group linking Brazil’s development and industry ministry with the U.S. Commerce Department to examine tariff disputes and report within 30 days.</li><li>Security agenda: Lula said the leaders discussed organized crime and that Brazil delivered an English-language proposal on cooperation against arms trafficking and money laundering.</li><li>Brazil election: The meeting took place as Lula seeks a fourth, non-consecutive term in Brazil’s October election against Flávio Bolsonaro, son of Jair Bolsonaro.</li><li>Digital policy: Brazilian accounts said the talks also covered relations with large technology companies, while Planalto had viewed the trip as a chance to raise digital regulation.</li><li>Follow-up meetings: Trump said representatives from both governments had meetings scheduled on key issues and that further meetings could be arranged in the coming months.</li></ul><blockquote>Quem estiver errado vai ceder. Se a gente tiver que ceder, nós vamos ceder - President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: In later comments to reporters, Trump said Brazil wanted tariff relief, called Lula a “good man” and “smart guy,” and predicted bilateral trade would increase. (<a href='https://veja.abril.com.br/mundo/trump-volta-a-falar-de-reuniao-com-lula-e-elogia-petista/'>Veja</a>)</li><li>President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva: Lula said he left the meeting satisfied and said Trump’s visible good humor was a positive sign, adding that a laughing Trump was better than a serious one. (<a href='https://jornaldebrasilia.com.br/noticias/politica-e-poder/com-tarifas-guerra-copa-e-amor-a-primeira-vista-entenda-a-reuniao-de-lula-e-trump/'>Jornal de Brasília</a>)</li><li>Amcham Brasil: The American Chamber of Commerce for Brazil called the meeting positive but said working-level talks still needed to advance. (<a href='https://valor.globo.com/brasil/noticia/2026/05/07/encontro-de-lula-e-trump-foi-positivo-mas-reunies-de-trabalho-precisam-avanar-diz-amcham-brasil.ghtml'>Valor</a>)</li><li>Rubens Ricupero: The former Brazilian ambassador to Washington said Trump’s respectful treatment of Lula made a normal meeting look unusual, but assessed the concrete result as limited. (<a href='https://www.cartacapital.com.br/politica/ricupero-respeito-de-trump-a-lula-fez-reuniao-normal-soar-extraord